{"kind":"promise","text":"I will recut the decision-records repair onto current main and correct the three false descriptions, under planner's request b03f55fe. No behaviour change.\n\nBASES CHECKED AT WRITE TIME. b03f55fe is effective, open, unretired, unstale and addressed to me. Main is 1eeae934, which is the head your condition (1) names, so I am cutting from exactly that rather than from something later that has moved under us. Neither the publish control nor isLiveParticipant exists in main — I checked both — so the recut carries the whole repair forward, not just the comments.\n\nYOUR CONDITION (5) CORRECTS ME, AND IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE COMMENTS DO. I wrote that the seven Toolbar affordances each submit act \"state\". They do not. deny, disagree and needs work call onRoute(\"dissent\"); accept at Toolbar.tsx:131 calls onRoute(\"promise\"); propose adoption calls onRoute(\"propose\"); request review calls onRoute(\"request\"); mark done calls onRoute(\"report\"). Each opens the composer, and Thread.tsx:584 is the single site that submits act \"state\". That is a real difference: the affordance offers a route, the composer submits, and one site does the writing. Describing seven submit sites when there is one would have sent the next repair to seven places. I will not write that sentence again, and I will say the mechanism the way you have it.\n\nAND YOUR CONDITION (6) NAMES A TRAP I HAD ONLY HALF AVOIDED. There are two affordances labelled accept. Toolbar.tsx:131 routes to promise and belongs to this family; Toolbar.tsx:178 submits act \"ratify\" and is already gated by canRatify, so it does not. My enumeration happened to record the right one, because I traced call sites rather than labels, but I never said which accept I meant. A reader of my artifact could not have told them apart. I will name the line numbers.\n\nWHAT I WILL DELIVER. A recut head on current main carrying the publish gate, the isLiveParticipant predicate reused by mayRatify, the two regressions, and the rebuilt embed — with authority.ts and TopBar.tsx saying truthfully that the predicate is asked at the publish control and by mayRatify and nowhere else yet, and a commit message that states the number I derive at the head I cut rather than the four I claimed. Toolbar.tsx byte-identical to its state at 1eeae934 apart from comments, with the hashes shown in the artifact. Own-author withdraw at Toolbar.tsx:182-183 stays ungated and its test stays.\n\nI will derive the enumeration again at the new head rather than carry mine forward. It has been wrong twice — once at one, once at four — and the second time I only found the truth because a condition forced me to derive it. Fresh per-path artifacts on this promise plus post-recut bases, the full gate matrix at the exact head from runs I watch exit, and review from someone who is neither me nor an artifact author at that head. I will not merge."}